## 4. Execution model

{% if scheduler == "local" %}
- This run used the **local scheduler** (no Slurm): `run.py` ran the modules **serially** in one process
  (`confluence/utils/local_runner.py`, log `log/{{ run_name }}_local-<epoch>.out`), starting each task as `bash sh_scripts/<module>.sh` with
  the environment Slurm would set. Continent/global modules are one "array" (one task per continent in
  `input/continent.json`, or a single task).
- Per-reach modules: the entries of the module's index JSON are split into chunks of `reach_chunks`
  ({{ ctx.local.reach_chunks }}; a module's own `reach_chunks` overrides it) consecutive indices per task. There are no batches:
  after the canary, a module's tasks are one array, and at most `local.concurrent_jobs`
  ({{ ctx.local.concurrent_jobs or "one per CPU core" }}{% if ctx.local.module_concurrency %}; per module: {{ ctx.local.module_concurrency }}{% endif %}) run at once;
  `python -m confluence.monitor throttle` changes that limit while the run is going. Each task handles
  `idx = OFFSET + SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID * INDEX_RANGE … + INDEX_RANGE - 1`, clamped to `MAX_LIMIT - 1`. Array ids are
  `local-<epoch>-<n>`; sacct has no record of them.
{% else %}
- `sh_scripts/slurm_driver.sh` (job `confluence_driver_{{ run_name }}`) runs the modules **serially**. Continent/global
  modules are one array (one task per continent in `input/continent.json`, or a single task).
- Per-reach modules: the entries of the module's index JSON are split into chunks of `reach_chunks`
  ({{ ctx.hpc.reach_chunks }}; a module's own `reach_chunks` overrides it) consecutive indices per array task. Arrays hold at most `batch_size` ({{ ctx.hpc.batch_size }}) tasks,
  and at most `concurrent_jobs` ({{ ctx.hpc.concurrent_jobs }}) of them run at once; `python -m confluence.monitor throttle`
  changes that limit for the running driver (recorded as `concurrent_jobs a -> b` in `monitor/events.log`). Each task handles
  `idx = OFFSET + SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID * INDEX_RANGE … + INDEX_RANGE - 1`, clamped to `MAX_LIMIT - 1`.
{% endif %}
- Index files: `input/expanded_reaches_of_interest.json` (input), `input/metrosets.json` (metroman), `input/basin.json` (moi),
  `input/continent.json` (continent modules), `input/reaches.json` (everything else).
{% if scheduler == "local" %}
- With the monitor enabled, a **gate** checks each module after its small **canary** array (only if
  `monitor.canary_tasks` > 0; off by default) and after the whole module, and writes `monitor/ABORT` on systemic failure.
  Nothing watches tasks in flight (that needs squeue), and the run is audited at its end unless it was interrupted.
{% else %}
- With the monitor enabled, a **gate** (`python -m confluence.monitor gate`) checks each module after its small
  **canary** array (only if `monitor.canary_tasks` > 0; off by default), after each batch and after the whole module, and writes
  `monitor/ABORT` on systemic failure. The **monitor**, a background process of the driver job (log
  `log/monitor_<driver jobid>.out`), watches arrays in flight and keeps `monitor/status.txt` current. An **audit job**
  (`audit_{{ run_name }}_cfl`, queued with `--dependency=afterany` on the driver) runs the audit however the driver ends.
{% endif %}
- Every container call is wrapped by `cfl_unit` (`sh_scripts/cfl_lib.sh`), which records BEGIN/END + exit code per unit in
  `monitor/units/<module>/<array>_<task>.tsv` (columns: event module array task idx unit rc epoch secs node) and prints
  `CFL_UNIT_BEGIN/END` markers into the task log. Task exit codes: 0 all units ok, 3 some failed, 4 all failed.
{% if not has_ledger %}
- **This run predates the monitor:** there is no `submissions.tsv` or unit ledger, task exit codes only reflect the last reach of
  each chunk, and logs are named `<module>.<task job id>_<task>.out`. Treat sacct states as unreliable.
{% endif %}

